    "last_text": "\u0001\n618 \nFXUS64 KLCH 031732\nAFDLCH\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Lake Charles LA\n1232 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\n...New DISCUSSION, AVIATION, MARINE, FIRE WEATHER...\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- Dewpoints will begin to rise through the work week, while both\n  high and low temperatures are forecast to remain at or slightly\n  above average.\n\n-Isolated diurnally driven afternoon thunderstorms will be\n possible each day through the work week, with rain chances in the\n 15 to 30% range.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\n(This evening through next Sunday)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nYesterday's weak boundary remains draped along the I-10 corridor \nthis afternoon, providing light and variable winds across the \nforecast area. The boundary will continue its slow meander north \ntowards the Ark-La-Miss through the early work week, allowing \nonshore flow to eventually become re-established once again. This \nwill allow for a slow but steady increase in moisture through the \nnext couple of days, with PWATs expected to return to near the \n50th percentile (~1.80\" according to SPC climo) by Wednesday. \nAloft, troughing over the E CONUS will gradually lift north today,\nleaving behind a weak cutoff low over the SE CONUS. This low will\nlinger nearby into Wednesday, preventing upper ridging from \ntaking hold for a while longer. With some additional upper level \nsupport nearby, a slight increase in afternoon rain chances is \nexpected tomorrow and Wed, especially across the eastern parts of \nthe CWA closer to the influence of the upper low.\n\nBy Thursday the upper low will be fully dissipated, with upper \nridging over both the Four Corners region and the western Atlantic \nbeginning to strengthen to our west and east simultaneously. Still, \nat this point guidance is in good agreement that these two ridges \nwon't quite reach each other until possibly beyond the end of this \nforecast period, leaving a small weakness between them overhead \nthrough next weekend. At the surface, a well established onshore \nflow around a SE CONUS surface high will allow for even better \nmoisture return through this same period, with PWATs increasing \ninto/above the 75th percentile range. With decent moisture available \nand a slight weakness aloft, the chance for afternoon showers and \nstorms will increase a bit further each day Thurs through Sun. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...\n(18Z TAFS)\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nScattered to broken cloud deck near 5000 ft will continue to\nstream across the region into this evening, thinking\nout/dissipating overnight. Light and variable winds near 0-5kts \nwill continue at all sites through the period. \n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nA light west to southwest flow will continue through the next couple \nof days, becoming more southerly by late Tuesday and beyond. A few \nisolated showers will be possible later in the period, but overall \nmostly dry conditions are expected through the work week.\n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nIssued at 1229 PM CDT Mon Aug 3 2026\n\nMinimum afternoon RH values are expected to range from 35 to 55 \npercent once again today, amid a light and variable onshore flow. \nMoisture begins to slowly return today through the mid-week, \nallowing min RH values to return to the 50-60% range each day. \nIncreasing moisture will bring back a chance for isolated afternoon \nthunderstorms again Tuesday and beyond.\n\n&&\n\n.LCH WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nLA...None.\nTX...None.\nGM...None.\n&&\n\n$$\n\nPUBLIC/FIRE/MARINE...17\nAVIATION...17\n\n\u0003\n",
